# Round boltfold, P2: the same one-trace profile as P1 (docs/cards/boltfold-p1.card) on the binary that carries H1 (the MoE layer's two output adds as one add3, 40 launches
# fewer) and F1 (the shared expert's chain on a Branch stream beside the router and the routed chain). P1 measured, on the base: 900 nodes, one stream, replay wall 5,252.1 us =
# kernel sum 5,089.7 + gaps 162.7, `add` slot 13 20.3 us a step, shared chain B 480.9 us a step (12.02 us a layer) against the routed chain's 36.26 us a layer (B/A 0.33).
# Derived replay wall of the new tree: 5,252.1 - H1 (20.3 us of kernel + 40 x 0.18 us of gap - ~1 us for the third operand's read = 26.5 us) - the part of B the second stream
# hides. B is 480.9 us a step at most (no contention). A hidden share of 25..75 % of it (the fork and join edges cost the rest; B's gemvs contend with A's up and down gemvs for
# HBM, and run free under the router and the latent down, grids of 60..124 and 32 blocks on 170 SMs) gives 120..361 us, so the wall is 5,252.1 - 26.5 - (120..361) = 4,864..5,106 us.
# READ AFTER THE RUN (2026-11-28 10:04 KST, sit-p2.log; base and new in one lease, 79 s): wall 5,253.3 us (base, 900 nodes, 1 stream) -> 4,923.8 us (new, 860 nodes, 2 streams) = in the
# predicted band 4,864..5,106; decide-in. Two conditions below were wrong on paper and are struck here, not hidden: (1) "the `add` class 88 launches and an `add3` class of 40" counted the
# 40 add3 twice: the new binary runs 48 `add` + 40 `add3` = 88 add-class launches (the base's 128 less 40). (2) "the kernel sum 5,040..5,110 us" assumed the kernels keep their
# durations: the new trace's kernel sum is 5,318.3 us because two streams overlap and stretch each other (router 508.0 -> 662.4, iq2_xxs_gemv_sel 882.3 -> 932.1, q8_0_gemv 953.5 -> 965.6,
# relu quantize 108.8 -> 130.3); the wall, not the sum, is the term, and the stretch is the contention the paper priced as the 25..75 % of B the second stream does not hide (63 % hid).
kind: profile
question: does the shared expert's chain run beside the routed chain in the captured Bolt step (two streams in a replay, 860 nodes), and how much of the base's 5,252.1 us
  replay wall do the add3 fold and the fork take off?
term: the replay wall (first kernel start to last kernel end of one graph replay, median over the replays after the first two) of the new tree's bloomery-serve (nsys
  cuda-graph-trace=node, serving defaults, whole-card Bolt 24GB file, D=512 prose ids, n=128, temperature 0, RTX 5090 at 600 W), read against the base's 5,252.1 us from the same
  tooling and conditions (p1.md)
unit: us
predict: 4864..5106
condition: one lease through tools/ref/lease-hold.sh --card, under the LEAD-TIMING-HOLD, the card idle (apps-before empty), ComfyUI not running; the new binary's sha256 recorded in
  the log; the replay size must read 860 (858..862: the base's 900 less one add per MoE layer) on at least 100 replays; streams in a replay = 2; the `add` class 88 launches
  and an `add3` class of 40; the kernel sum 5,040..5,110 us (base 5,089.7 less slot 13's 20.3 us, plus ~1 us); nsys adds a few percent to a wall, so only the ratio to the base's
  5,252.1 us from the same tooling is read
decide-in: 4,864..5,106 us with two streams: the fork is in the graph and hides part of B as the paper says; the same-lease A/B (depth-serve base vs new, D=512, n=256) is the next
  run, its card band derived from this wall (effect = 5,252.1 / wall - 1)
decide-below: below 4,864 us: more than B's 75 % hidden on top of H1, which the ceiling (H1 26.5 + B 480.9 = 507 us, wall 4,745) allows only to 4,745: read the kernel sum and the
  node count first (a missing launch would make a fast wrong step), then the gates, before any claim
decide-above: above 5,106 us, or one stream in the replay: the fork hides under 25 % of B or is not in the graph. One stream: print each layer's `fork` at load (forks_apart of the
  file's widths) and the Branch's capture dependencies before anything else. Two streams: read the per-kernel start times of one layer in the sqlite (where B's three kernels
  start relative to the router and the latent down) and move the fork point (before the router, after the latent down) with a P3 trace; no A/B before the wall moves
